---
title: "Nix build aliases: one result per request"
date: 2026-07-11
---

The runtime CVE scanner requested 375 security roots in one
`nix build --json` call and received 377 results. Every root evaluated
correctly alone. The failure appeared only when two names selected the same
derived path.

## Root cause

Pinned Nix 2.34.7 collected every requested derived path and also kept a
backmap from each path to its requesting installables. The store returned one
result for every input path, including duplicates. Nix then expanded each
duplicate result through every matching backmap entry. Two aliases therefore
became four result rows.

The affected scanner roots were `nushell` and
`nushell-aarch64-apple-darwin`. A smaller reproduction made the cardinality
error explicit:

```sh
nix build --no-link --json nixpkgs#hello nixpkgs#coreutils nixpkgs#hello | jq length
# 5, expected 3
```

## Fix

The index Nix patch now separates requests from build targets. It deduplicates
targets by `DerivedPath`, realizes each target once, then maps the realized
result back to every original request in request order. The upstream-style
functional regression asks for `a`, `b`, then `a` and requires exactly those
three ordered JSON rows.

This fixes the format at its owner. The scanner keeps its strict cardinality
check and needs no retry, alias heuristic, or result-shape fallback.

Issue #2633.

